## an10.94
an10.94:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Campā on the banks of the Gaggarā Lotus Pond. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Campā on the shore of Gaggarā Lake.
an10.94:1.2 | S: Then the householder Vajjiyamāhita left Sāvatthī in the middle of the day to see the Buddha. | T: Then Vajjiya Māhita the householder left Campā in the middle of the day to see the Blessed One,
an10.94:1.3 | S: Then it occurred to him, | T: but then the thought occurred to him,
an10.94:1.4 | S: “It’s the wrong time to see the Buddha, | T: “Now is not the right time to see the Blessed One,
an10.94:1.5 | S: as he’s in retreat. | T: for he is in seclusion.
an10.94:1.6 | S: And it’s the wrong time to see the esteemed mendicants, | T: And it is not the right time to see the mind-developing monks,
an10.94:1.7 | S: as they’re in retreat. | T: for they too are in seclusion.
an10.94:1.8 | S: Why don’t I visit the monastery for the wanderers of other religions?” | T: Why don’t I visit the park of the wanderers of other persuasions?”
an10.94:2.1 | S: Then he went to the monastery for the wanderers of other religions. | T: So he headed to the park of the wanderers of other persuasions.
an10.94:2.2 | S: Now at that time, the wanderers of other religions had come together, making an uproar, a colossal racket as they sat and talked about all kinds of low topics. | T: Now on that occasion the wanderers of other persuasions had come together in a gathering and were sitting, discussing many kinds of bestial topics, making a great noise & racket.
an10.94:3.1 | S: They saw Vajjiyamāhita coming off in the distance, and stopped each other, saying, | T: They saw Vajjiya Māhita the householder coming from afar, and on seeing him, hushed one another:
an10.94:3.2 | S: “Be quiet, good fellows, don’t make a sound. | T: “Be quiet, good sirs. Don’t make any noise.
an10.94:3.3 | S: The householder Vajjiyamāhita, a disciple of the ascetic Gotama, is coming into our monastery. | T: Here comes Vajjiya Māhita the householder, a disciple of the contemplative Gotama.
an10.94:3.4 | S: He is included among the white-clothed lay disciples of the ascetic Gotama, who is residing near Campā. | T: He is one of those disciples of the contemplative Gotama, clad in white, who lives in Campā.
an10.94:3.5 | S: Such venerables like the quiet, are educated to be quiet, and praise the quiet. | T: These people are fond of quietude, trained in quietude, and speak in praise of quietude.
an10.94:3.6 | S: Hopefully if he sees that our assembly is quiet he’ll see fit to approach.” | T: Maybe, if he perceives our group as quiet, he will consider it worth his while to come our way.”
an10.94:4.1 | S: Then those wanderers of other religions fell silent. | T: So the wanderers fell silent.
an10.94:4.2 | S: Then Vajjiyamāhita went up to them, and exchanged greetings with the wanderers there. | T: Then Vajjiya Māhita the householder went to where the wanderers of other persuasions were staying. On arrival he greeted them courteously.
an10.94:4.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side. The wanderers said to him: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, the wanderers said to him,
an10.94:4.4 | S: “Is it really true, householder? Does the ascetic Gotama criticize all forms of mortification? Does he categorically condemn and denounce those fervent mortifiers who live rough?” | T: “Is it true, householder, that the contemplative Gotama criticizes all asceticism, that he categorically denounces & disparages all ascetics who live the rough life?”
an10.94:4.5 | S: “No, sirs, the Buddha does not criticize all forms of mortification. Nor does he categorically condemn and denounce those fervent mortifiers who live rough. | T: “No, venerable sirs, the Blessed One does not criticize all asceticism, nor does he categorically denounce or disparage all ascetics who live the rough life.
an10.94:4.6 | S: The Buddha criticizes where it is due, and praises where it is due. | T: The Blessed One criticizes what should be criticized, and praises what should be praised.
an10.94:4.7 | S: In doing so he is one who speaks after analyzing the question, | T: Criticizing what should be criticized, praising what should be praised, the Blessed One is one who speaks making distinctions,
an10.94:4.8 | S: and is not one-sided on this point.” | T: not one who speaks categorically on this matter.”
an10.94:5.1 | S: When he said this, one of the wanderers said to him, | T: When this was said, one of the wanderers said to Vajjiya Māhita the householder,
an10.94:5.2 | S: “Hold on, householder! That ascetic Gotama who you praise is an exterminator who refrains from making statements.” | T: “Now wait a minute, householder. This contemplative Gotama whom you praise is a nihilist, one who doesn’t declare anything.”
an10.94:5.3 | S: “On this point, also, I reasonably respond to the venerables. | T: “I tell you, venerable sirs,
an10.94:5.4 | S: The Buddha has stated ‘This is skillful’ and | T: that the Blessed One righteously declares that ‘This is skillful.’
an10.94:5.5 | S: ‘This is unskillful’. | T: He declares that ‘This is unskillful.’
an10.94:5.6 | S: So when it comes to what is skillful and unskillful the Buddha makes a statement. | T: Declaring that ‘This is skillful’ and ‘This is unskillful,’ he is one who has declared (a teaching).
an10.94:5.7 | S: He is not an exterminator who refrains from making statements.” | T: He is not a nihilist, one who doesn’t declare anything.”
an10.94:6.1 | S: When this was said, those wanderers sat silent, dismayed, shoulders drooping, downcast, depressed, with nothing to say. | T: When this was said, the wanderers fell silent, abashed, sitting with their shoulders drooping, their heads down, brooding, at a loss for words.
an10.94:6.2 | S: Seeing this, Vajjiyamāhita rose from his seat. He went to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, | T: Vajjiya Māhita the householder, perceiving that the wanderers were silent, abashed…at a loss for words, got up & went to the Blessed One. On arrival, having bowed down to the Blessed One, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there,
an10.94:6.3 | S: and informed the Buddha of all they had discussed. | T: he told the Blessed One the entirety of his conversation with the wanderers. (The Blessed One said:)
an10.94:7.1 | S: “Good, good, householder! | T: “Well done, householder. Well done.
an10.94:7.2 | S: That’s how you should legitimately and completely refute those futile men from time to time. | T: That is how you should periodically & righteously refute those foolish men.
an10.94:7.3 | S: Householder, I don’t say that all mortifications should be undergone. | T: I don’t say that all asceticism is to be pursued,
an10.94:7.4 | S: But I don’t say that no mortifications should be undergone. | T: nor do I say that all asceticism is not to be pursued.
an10.94:7.5 | S: I don’t say that all observances should be undertaken. | T: I don’t say that all observances should be observed,
an10.94:7.6 | S: But I don’t say that no observances should be undertaken. | T: nor do I say that all observances should not be observed.
an10.94:7.7 | S: I don’t say that all efforts should be tried. | T: I don’t say that all exertions are to be pursued,
an10.94:7.8 | S: But I don’t say that no efforts should be tried. | T: nor do I say that all exertions are not to be pursued.
an10.94:7.9 | S: I don’t say that everything should be given up. | T: I don’t say that all forfeiture should be forfeited,
an10.94:7.10 | S: But I don’t say that nothing should be given up. | T: nor do I say that all forfeiture should not be forfeited.
an10.94:7.11 | S: I don’t say that you should be liberated with all kinds of freedom. | T: I don’t say that all release is to be used for release,
an10.94:7.12 | S: But I don’t say that you should not be liberated with any kind of freedom. | T: nor do I say that all release is not to be used for release.
an10.94:8.1 | S: When undergoing certain mortifications, unskillful qualities grow while skillful qualities decline. I say that you shouldn’t undergo those mortifications. | T: “If, when an asceticism is pursued, unskillful qualities grow and skillful qualities wane, then I tell you that that sort of asceticism is not to be pursued.
an10.94:8.2 | S: When undergoing certain mortifications, unskillful qualities decline while skillful qualities grow. I say that you should undergo those mortifications. | T: But if, when an asceticism is pursued, unskillful qualities wane and skillful qualities grow, then I tell you that that sort of asceticism is to be pursued.
an10.94:9.1 | S: When undertaking certain observances, unskillful qualities grow while skillful qualities decline. I say that you shouldn’t undertake those observances. | T: “If, when an observance is observed, unskillful qualities grow and skillful qualities wane, then I tell you that that sort of observance is not to be observed.
an10.94:9.2 | S: When undertaking certain observances, unskillful qualities decline while skillful qualities grow. I say that you should undertake those observances. | T: But if, when an observance is observed, unskillful qualities wane and skillful qualities grow, then I tell you that that sort of observance is to be observed.
an10.94:10.1 | S: When trying certain efforts, unskillful qualities grow while skillful qualities decline. I say that you shouldn’t try those efforts. | T: “If, when an exertion is pursued.… a forfeiture is forfeited…
an10.94:10.2 | S: When trying certain efforts, unskillful qualities decline while skillful qualities grow. I say that you should try those efforts. | T: (none)
an10.94:11.1 | S: When giving up certain things, unskillful qualities grow while skillful qualities decline. I say that you shouldn’t give up those things. | T: (none)
an10.94:11.2 | S: When giving up certain things, unskillful qualities decline while skillful qualities grow. I say that you should give up those things. | T: (none)
an10.94:12.1 | S: When being liberated with certain kinds of freedom, unskillful qualities grow while skillful qualities decline. I say that you shouldn’t be liberated with those kinds of freedom. | T: “If, when a release is used for release, unskillful qualities grow and skillful qualities wane, then I tell you that that sort of release is not to be used for release.
an10.94:12.2 | S: When being liberated with certain kinds of freedom, unskillful qualities decline while skillful qualities grow. I say that you should be liberated with those kinds of freedom.” | T: But if, when a release is used for release, unskillful qualities wane and skillful qualities grow, then I tell you that that sort of release is to be used for release.”
an10.94:13.1 | S: After Vajjiyamāhita had been educated, encouraged, fired up, and inspired with a Dhamma talk by the Buddha, he got up from his seat, bowed, and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on his right, before leaving. | T: When Vajjiya Māhita the householder had been instructed, urged, roused & encouraged by the Blessed One with a talk on Dhamma, he got up from his seat and, having bowed down to the Blessed One, left, keeping the Blessed One on his right side.
an10.94:14.1 | S: Then, not long after Vajjiyamāhita had left, the Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: Not long afterward, the Blessed One addressed the monks:
an10.94:14.2 | S: “Mendicants, even a mendicant who for a long time has had little dust in their eye in this teaching and training would legitimately and completely refute those wanderers of other religions just as the householder Vajjiyamāhita did.” | T: “Monks, even a monk who has long penetrated the Dhamma in this Dhamma & Vinaya would do well, periodically & righteously, to refute the wanderers of other persuasions in just the way Vajjiya Māhita the householder has done.”
an10.94:14.3 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.52
an3.52:1.1 | S: Then two old brahmins—elderly and senior, who were advanced in years and had reached the final stage of life, a hundred and twenty years old—went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. Then two brahmans—feeble old men, aged, advanced in years, having come to the last stage of life, 120 years old—went to the Blessed One. On arrival, they exchanged courteous greetings with him and, after an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, sat to one side. As they were sitting there, they said to him:
an3.52:1.2 | S: “We brahmins, worthy Gotama, are old, elderly and senior, we’re advanced in years and have reached the final stage of life; we’re a hundred and twenty years old. | T: “Master Gotama, we are brahmans—feeble old men, aged, advanced in years, having come to the last stage of life, 120 years old.
an3.52:1.3 | S: And we haven’t done what is good and skillful, nor have we made a shelter from fear. | T: And we have done no admirable deeds, no skillful deeds, no deeds that allay our fears.
an3.52:1.4 | S: Advise us, worthy Gotama, instruct us! It will be for our lasting welfare and happiness.” | T: Teach us, Master Gotama. Instruct us, Master Gotama, for our long-term benefit & happiness.”
an3.52:2.1 | S: “Indeed, brahmins, you’re old, elderly and senior. | T: “Indeed, brahmans, you are feeble old men, aged, advanced in years, having come to the last stage of life, 120 years old.
an3.52:2.2 | S: And you haven’t done what is good and skillful, nor have you made a shelter from fear. | T: And you have done no admirable deeds, no skillful deeds, no deeds that allay your fears.
an3.52:2.3 | S: This world is burning with old age, sickness, and death. | T: This world is on fire with aging, illness, & death.
an3.52:2.4 | S: But restraint here by way of body, speech, and mind is the shelter, asylum, island, refuge, and final haven for the departed. | T: With the world thus on fire with aging, illness, & death, any restraint of body, speech, & intellect practiced here will be one’s shelter, cave, island, & refuge after death in the world beyond.”
an3.52:3.1 | S: When your house is on fire, | T: When a house is aflame,
an3.52:3.2 | S: you rescue the pot | T: the vessel salvaged
an3.52:3.3 | S: that’s useful, | T: is the one that will be of use,
an3.52:3.4 | S: not the one that’s burnt. | T: not the one left there to burn.
an3.52:4.1 | S: And as the world is on fire | T: So when the world is on fire
an3.52:4.2 | S: with old age and death, | T: with aging & death,
an3.52:4.3 | S: you should rescue by giving, | T: you should salvage (your wealth) by giving:
an3.52:4.4 | S: for what’s given is rescued. | T: What’s given is well salvaged.
an3.52:5.1 | S: The restraint practiced here— | T: When you’re restrained here
an3.52:5.2 | S: of body, speech, and mind— | T: in body, speech, & awareness;
an3.52:5.3 | S: leads the departed to happiness, | T: when you make merit while still alive: That will be for your bliss after death.
an3.52:5.4 | S: as the good deeds done while living.” | T: (none)
an3.52:5.5 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.72
an3.72:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Ānanda was staying near Kosambī in Ghosita’s monastery. | T: I have heard that on one occasion Ven. Ānanda was staying near Kosambī in Ghosita’s monastery.
an3.72:1.2 | S: Then a householder who was a disciple of the Ājīvaka ascetics went up to Venerable Ānanda, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to Ānanda: | T: Then a certain householder, a disciple of the Fatalists [Ājīvakas], went to him and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to Ven. Ānanda,
an3.72:2.1 | S: “Honorable Ānanda, whose teaching is well explained? | T: “Among us, sir, whose Dhamma is well-taught?
an3.72:2.2 | S: Who in the world is practicing well? | T: Who has practiced well in this world?
an3.72:2.3 | S: Who in the world has done well?” | T: Who in the world is Well-Gone?”
an3.72:2.4 | S: “Well then, householder, I’ll ask you about this in return, and you can answer as you like. | T: “In that case, householder, I will question you in return. Answer as you see fit.
an3.72:2.5 | S: What do you think, householder? | T: Now, what do you think?
an3.72:2.6 | S: Is the teaching of those who teach for giving up greed, hate, and delusion well explained or not? | T: Those who teach a Dhamma for the abandoning of passion, for the abandoning of aversion, for the abandoning of delusion—is their Dhamma well-taught or not?
an3.72:2.7 | S: Or how do you see this?” | T: Or how does this strike you?”
an3.72:2.8 | S: “The teaching of those who teach for giving up greed, hate, and delusion is well explained. | T: “Sir, those who teach a Dhamma for the abandoning of passion, for the abandoning of aversion, for the abandoning of delusion—their Dhamma is well-taught.
an3.72:2.9 | S: That’s how I see it.” | T: That’s how it strikes me.”
an3.72:3.1 | S: “What do you think, householder? | T: “And what do you think, householder?
an3.72:3.2 | S: Are those who practice for giving up greed, hate, and delusion practicing well or not? | T: Those who have practiced for the abandoning of passion, for the abandoning of aversion, for the abandoning of delusion—have they practiced well in this world or not?
an3.72:3.3 | S: Or how do you see this?” | T: Or how does this strike you?”
an3.72:3.4 | S: “Those who practice for giving up greed, hate, and delusion are practicing well. | T: “Sir, those who have practiced for the abandoning of passion, for the abandoning of aversion, for the abandoning of delusion—they have practiced well in this world.
an3.72:3.5 | S: That’s how I see it.” | T: That’s how it strikes me.”
an3.72:4.1 | S: “What do you think, householder? | T: “And what do you think, householder?
an3.72:4.2 | S: Have those who’ve given up greed, hate, and delusion—so they’re cut off at the root, made like a palm stump, obliterated, and unable to arise in the future—done well in the world, or not? | T: Those whose passion is abandoned, its root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising; those whose aversion is abandoned… whose delusion is abandoned, its root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising—are they, in this world, Well-Gone or not?
an3.72:4.3 | S: Or how do you see this?” | T: Or how does this strike you?”
an3.72:4.4 | S: “Those who’ve given up greed, hate, and delusion have done well in the world. | T: “Sir, those whose passion… aversion… delusion is abandoned, its root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising—they, in this world, are Well-Gone.
an3.72:4.5 | S: That’s how I see it.” | T: That’s how it strikes me.”
an3.72:5.1 | S: “So, householder, you’ve declared: | T: “In this way, householder, you have answered yourself:
an3.72:5.2 | S: ‘The teaching of those who teach for giving up greed, hate, and delusion is well explained.’ | T: ‘Those who teach a Dhamma for the abandoning of passion, for the abandoning of aversion, for the abandoning of delusion—their Dhamma is well-taught.
an3.72:5.3 | S: And you’ve declared: | T: (none)
an3.72:5.4 | S: ‘Those who practice for giving up greed, hate, and delusion are practicing well.’ | T: Those who have practiced for the abandoning of passion, for the abandoning of aversion, for the abandoning of delusion—they have practiced well in this world.
an3.72:5.5 | S: And you’ve declared: | T: (none)
an3.72:5.6 | S: ‘Those who’ve given up greed, hate, and delusion have done well in the world.’” | T: Those whose passion… aversion… delusion is abandoned, its root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising—they, in this world, are Well-Gone.’”
an3.72:6.1 | S: “It’s incredible, sir, it’s amazing! | T: “How amazing, sir. How astounding,
an3.72:6.2 | S: There’s no acclaiming your own teaching or disrespecting someone else’s, | T: that there is neither extolling of one’s own Dhamma nor deprecation of another’s,
an3.72:6.3 | S: just teaching what’s relevant in that context. The goal is spoken of, but the self is not involved. | T: but just the teaching of the Dhamma in its proper sphere, speaking to the point without mentioning oneself.
an3.72:6.4 | S: You, sir, teach Dhamma for giving up greed, hate, and delusion. | T: “You, venerable sir, teach the Dhamma for the abandoning of passion… aversion… delusion.
an3.72:6.5 | S: Your teaching is well explained. | T: Your Dhamma is well-taught.
an3.72:6.6 | S: You, sir, practice for giving up greed, hate, and delusion. | T: You have practiced for the abandoning of passion… aversion… delusion.
an3.72:6.7 | S: You in the world are practicing well. | T: You have practiced well in this world.
an3.72:6.8 | S: You’ve given up greed, hate, and delusion. | T: Your passion… aversion… delusion is abandoned, its root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising.
an3.72:6.9 | S: You in the world have done well. | T: You, in this world, are Well-Gone.
an3.72:7.1 | S: Excellent, sir! Excellent! | T: “Magnificent, Master Ānanda! Magnificent!
an3.72:7.2 | S: As if he were righting the overturned, or revealing the hidden, or pointing out the path to the lost, or lighting a lamp in the dark so people with clear eyes can see what’s there, Master Ānanda has made the teaching clear in many ways. | T: Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has Master Ānanda—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear.
an3.72:7.3 | S: I go for refuge to the Buddha, to the teaching, and to the mendicant Saṅgha. | T: I go to the Buddha for refuge, to the Dhamma, & to the Saṅgha of monks.

