an3.72:7.4 | S: From this day forth, may Master Ānanda remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.” | T: May Master Ānanda remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge from this day forward, for life.”
an3.72:7.5 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.200
an4.200:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, these four things are born. | T: “Monks, these four things are born.
an4.200:1.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.200:1.3 | S: Fondness is born of fondness, | T: Love is born of love.
an4.200:1.4 | S: hate is born of fondness, | T: Aversion is born of love.
an4.200:1.5 | S: fondness is born of hate, and | T: Love is born of aversion.
an4.200:1.6 | S: hate is born of hate. | T: Aversion is born of aversion.
an4.200:2.1 | S: And how is fondness born of fondness? | T: “And how is love born of love?
an4.200:2.2 | S: It’s when someone likes, loves, and cares for an individual. | T: There is the case where an individual is pleasing, appealing, & charming to (another) individual.
an4.200:2.3 | S: Others treat them with liking, love, and care. | T: Others treat that individual as pleasing, appealing, & charming,
an4.200:2.4 | S: They think: | T: and the other one thinks,
an4.200:2.5 | S: ‘These others like the individual I like.’ | T: ‘This individual is pleasing, appealing, & charming to me. Others treat this individual as pleasing, appealing, & charming.’
an4.200:2.6 | S: And so fondness for them springs up. | T: He gives rise to love for them.
an4.200:2.7 | S: That’s how fondness is born of fondness. | T: This is how love is born of love.
an4.200:3.1 | S: And how is hate born of fondness? | T: “And how is aversion born of love?
an4.200:3.2 | S: It’s when someone likes, loves, and cares for an individual. | T: There is the case where an individual is pleasing, appealing, & charming to (another) individual.
an4.200:3.3 | S: Others treat them with disliking, loathing, and detestation. | T: Others treat that individual as displeasing, unappealing, & not charming,
an4.200:3.4 | S: They think: | T: and the other one thinks,
an4.200:3.5 | S: ‘These others dislike the individual I like.’ | T: ‘This individual is pleasing, appealing, & charming to me. Others treat this individual as displeasing, unappealing, & not charming.’
an4.200:3.6 | S: And so hate for them springs up. | T: He gives rise to aversion for them.
an4.200:3.7 | S: That’s how hate is born of fondness. | T: This is how aversion is born of love.
an4.200:4.1 | S: And how is fondness born of hate? | T: “And how is love born of aversion?
an4.200:4.2 | S: It’s when someone dislikes, loathes, and detests an individual. | T: There is the case where an individual is displeasing, unappealing, & not charming to (another) individual.
an4.200:4.3 | S: Others treat them with disliking, loathing, and detestation. | T: Others treat that individual as displeasing, unappealing, & not charming,
an4.200:4.4 | S: They think: | T: and the other one thinks,
an4.200:4.5 | S: ‘These others dislike the individual I dislike.’ | T: ‘This individual is displeasing, unappealing, & not charming to me. Others treat this individual as displeasing, unappealing, & not charming.’
an4.200:4.6 | S: And so fondness for them springs up. | T: He gives rise to love for them.
an4.200:4.7 | S: That’s how fondness is born of hate. | T: This is how love is born of aversion.
an4.200:5.1 | S: And how is hate born of hate? | T: “And how is aversion born of aversion?
an4.200:5.2 | S: It’s when someone dislikes, loathes, and detests an individual. | T: There is the case where an individual is displeasing, unappealing, & not charming to (another) individual.
an4.200:5.3 | S: Others treat them with liking, love, and care. | T: Others treat that individual as pleasing, appealing, & charming,
an4.200:5.4 | S: They think: | T: and the other one thinks,
an4.200:5.5 | S: ‘These others like the individual I dislike.’ | T: ‘This individual is displeasing, unappealing, & not charming to me. Others treat this individual as pleasing, appealing, & charming.’
an4.200:5.6 | S: And so hate for them springs up. | T: He gives rise to aversion for them.
an4.200:5.7 | S: That’s how hate is born of hate. | T: This is how aversion is born of aversion.
an4.200:5.8 | S: These are the four things that are born. | T: “Monks, these are the four things that are born.
an4.200:6.1 | S: A time comes when a mendicant … enters and remains in the first absorption. | T: “Now, on the occasion when a monk, quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters & remains in the first jhāna—rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation—
an4.200:6.2 | S: At that time they have no fondness born of fondness, hate born of fondness, fondness born of hate, or hate born of hate. | T: then any love of his that is born of love does not come about. Any aversion of his that is born of love… any love of his that is born of aversion… any aversion of his that is born of aversion does not come about. “On the occasion when a monk, with the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, enters & remains in the second jhāna—rapture & pleasure born of concentration, unification of awareness free from directed thought & evaluation, internal assurance—then any love of his that is born of love does not come about. Any aversion of his that is born of love… any love of his that is born of aversion… any aversion of his that is born of aversion does not come about.
an4.200:7.1 | S: A time comes when a mendicant … enters and remains in the second absorption … third absorption … fourth absorption. | T: “On the occasion when a monk, with the fading of rapture, remains equanimous, mindful, & alert, senses pleasure with the body, and enters & remains in the third jhāna—of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous & mindful, he has a pleasant abiding’—
an4.200:7.2 | S: At that time they have no fondness born of fondness, hate born of fondness, fondness born of hate, or hate born of hate. | T: then any love of his that is born of love does not come about. Any aversion of his that is born of love… any love of his that is born of aversion… any aversion of his that is born of aversion does not come about. “On the occasion when a monk, with the abandoning of pleasure & pain, as with the earlier disappearance of elation & distress, enters & remains in the fourth jhāna—purity of equanimity & mindfulness, neither pleasure nor pain—then any love of his that is born of love does not come about. Any aversion of his that is born of love… any love of his that is born of aversion… any aversion of his that is born of aversion does not come about.
an4.200:8.1 | S: A time comes when a mendicant realizes the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. And they live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: “On the occasion when a monk, through the ending of effluents, enters & remains in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, having directly known & realized them for himself right in the here & now,
an4.200:8.2 | S: At that time any fondness born of fondness, hate born of fondness, fondness born of hate, or hate born of hate is given up, cut off at the root, made like a palm stump, obliterated, and unable to arise in the future. | T: then any love of his that is born of love is abandoned, its root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising. Any aversion of his that is born of love… any love of his that is born of aversion… any aversion of his that is born of aversion is abandoned, its root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising.
an4.200:8.3 | S: This is called a mendicant who doesn’t draw close or push back or fume or ignite or burn up. | T: “This is said to be a monk who doesn’t pull in, doesn’t push away, doesn’t smolder, doesn’t flare up, and doesn’t burn.
an4.200:9.1 | S: And how does a mendicant draw close? | T: “And how does a monk pull in?
an4.200:9.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant regards form as self, self as having form, form in self, or self in form. | T: There is the case where a monk assumes form to be the self, or the self as possessing form, or form as in the self, or the self as in form.
an4.200:9.3 | S: They regard feeling as self, self as having feeling, feeling in self, or self in feeling. | T: He assumes feeling to be the self, or the self as possessing feeling, or feeling as in the self, or the self as in feeling.
an4.200:9.4 | S: They regard perception as self, self as having perception, perception in self, or self in perception. | T: He assumes perception to be the self, or the self as possessing perception, or perception as in the self, or the self as in perception.
an4.200:9.5 | S: They regard choices as self, self as having choices, choices in self, or self in choices. | T: He assumes fabrications to be the self, or the self as possessing fabrications, or fabrications as in the self, or the self as in fabrications.
an4.200:9.6 | S: They regard consciousness as self, self as having consciousness, consciousness in self, or self in consciousness. | T: He assumes consciousness to be the self, or the self as possessing consciousness, or consciousness as in the self, or the self as in consciousness.
an4.200:9.7 | S: That’s how a mendicant draws close. | T: This is how a monk pulls in.
an4.200:10.1 | S: And how does a mendicant not draw close? | T: “And how does a monk not pull in?
an4.200:10.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t regard form as self, self as having form, form in self, or self in form. | T: There is the case where a monk doesn’t assume form to be the self, or the self as possessing form, or form as in the self, or the self as in form.
an4.200:10.3 | S: They don’t regard feeling as self, self as having feeling, feeling in self, or self in feeling. | T: He doesn’t assume feeling to be the self.…
an4.200:10.4 | S: They don’t regard perception as self, self as having perception, perception in self, or self in perception. | T: doesn’t assume perception to be the self.…
an4.200:10.5 | S: They don’t regard choices as self, self as having choices, choices in self, or self in choices. | T: doesn’t assume fabrications to be the self.…
an4.200:10.6 | S: They don’t regard consciousness as self, self as having consciousness, consciousness in self, or self in consciousness. | T: doesn’t assume consciousness to be the self, or the self as possessing consciousness, or consciousness as in the self, or the self as in consciousness.
an4.200:10.7 | S: That’s how a mendicant doesn’t draw close. | T: This is how a monk doesn’t pull in.
an4.200:11.1 | S: And how does a mendicant push back? | T: “And how does a monk push away?
an4.200:11.2 | S: It’s when someone abuses, annoys, or argues with a mendicant, and the mendicant abuses, annoys, or argues back at them. | T: There is the case where a monk returns insult to one who has insulted him, returns anger to one who is angry at him, quarrels with one who is quarreling.
an4.200:11.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant pushes back. | T: This is how a monk pushes away.
an4.200:12.1 | S: And how does a mendicant not push back? | T: “And how does a monk not push away?
an4.200:12.2 | S: It’s when someone abuses, annoys, or argues with a mendicant, and the mendicant doesn’t abuse, annoy, or argue back at them. | T: There is the case where a monk doesn’t return insult to one who has insulted him, doesn’t return anger to one who is angry at him, doesn’t quarrel with one who is quarreling.
an4.200:12.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant doesn’t push back. | T: This is how a monk doesn’t push away.
an4.200:13.1 | S: And how does a mendicant fume? | T: “And how does a monk smolder?
an4.200:13.2 | S: When there is the concept ‘I am’, there are the concepts ‘I am such’, ‘I am thus’, ‘I am otherwise’; ‘I am fleeting’, ‘I am lasting’; ‘mine’, ‘such is mine’, ‘thus is mine’, ‘otherwise is mine’; ‘also mine’, ‘such is also mine’, ‘thus is also mine’, ‘otherwise is also mine’; ‘I will be’, ‘I will be such’, ‘I will be thus’, ‘I will be otherwise’. | T: There is the case where, there being ‘I am,’ there comes to be ‘I am here,’ there comes to be ‘I am like this’ … ‘I am otherwise’ … ‘I am bad’ … ‘I am good’ … ‘I might be’ … ‘I might be here’ … ‘I might be like this’ … ‘I might be otherwise’ … ‘May I be’ … ‘May I be here’ … ‘May I be like this’ … ‘May I be otherwise’ … ‘I will be’ … ‘I will be here’ … ‘I will be like this’ … ‘I will be otherwise.’
an4.200:13.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant fumes. | T: (none)
an4.200:14.1 | S: And how does a mendicant not fume? | T: “And how does a monk not smolder?
an4.200:14.2 | S: When there is no concept ‘I am’, there are no concepts ‘I am such’, ‘I am thus’, ‘I am otherwise’; ‘I am fleeting’, ‘I am lasting’; ‘mine’, ‘such is mine’, ‘thus is mine’, ‘otherwise is mine’; ‘also mine’, ‘such is also mine’, ‘thus is also mine’, ‘otherwise is also mine’; ‘I will be’, ‘I will be such’, ‘I will be thus’, ‘I will be otherwise’. | T: There is the case where, there not being ‘I am,’ there doesn’t come to be ‘I am here,’ there doesn’t come to be ‘I am like this’ … ‘I am otherwise’ … ‘I am bad’ … ‘I am good’ … ‘I might be’ … ‘I might be here’ … ‘I might be like this’ … ‘I might be otherwise’ … ‘May I be’ … ‘May I be here’ … ‘May I be like this’ … ‘May I be otherwise’ … ‘I will be’ … ‘I will be here’ … ‘I will be like this’ … ‘I will be otherwise.’
an4.200:14.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant doesn’t fume. | T: (none)
an4.200:15.1 | S: And how is a mendicant ignited? | T: “And how does a monk flare up?
an4.200:15.2 | S: When there is the concept ‘I am because of this’, there are the concepts ‘I am such because of this’, ‘I am thus because of this’, ‘I am otherwise because of this’; ‘I am fleeting because of this’, ‘I am lasting because of this’; ‘mine because of this’, ‘such is mine because of this’, ‘thus is mine because of this’, ‘otherwise is mine because of this’; ‘also mine because of this’, ‘such is also mine because of this’, ‘thus is also mine because of this’, ‘otherwise is also mine because of this’; ‘I will be because of this’, ‘I will be such because of this’, ‘I will be thus because of this’, ‘I will be otherwise because of this’. | T: There is the case where, there being ‘I am because of this [or: by means of this],’ there comes to be ‘I am here because of this,’ there comes to be ‘I am like this because of this’ … ‘I am otherwise because of this’ … ‘I am bad because of this’ … ‘I am good because of this’ … ‘I might be because of this’ … ‘I might be here because of this’ … ‘I might be like this because of this’ … ‘I might be otherwise because of this’ … ‘May I be because of this’ … ‘May I be here because of this’ … ‘May I be like this because of this’ … ‘May I be otherwise because of this’ … ‘I will be because of this’ … ‘I will be here because of this’ … ‘I will be like this because of this’ … ‘I will be otherwise because of this.’
an4.200:15.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is ignited. | T: (none)
an4.200:16.1 | S: And how is a mendicant not ignited? | T: “And how does a monk not flare up?
an4.200:16.2 | S: When there is no concept ‘I am because of this’, there are no concepts ‘I am such because of this’, ‘I am thus because of this’, ‘I am otherwise because of this’; ‘I am fleeting because of this’, ‘I am lasting because of this’; ‘mine because of this’, ‘such is mine because of this’, ‘thus is mine because of this’, ‘otherwise is mine because of this’; ‘also mine because of this’, ‘such is also mine because of this’, ‘thus is also mine because of this’, ‘otherwise is also mine because of this’; ‘I will be because of this’, ‘I will be such because of this’, ‘I will be thus because of this’, ‘I will be otherwise because of this’. | T: There is the case where, there not being ‘I am because of this [or: by means of this],’ there doesn’t come to be ‘I am here because of this,’ there doesn’t come to be ‘I am like this because of this’ … ‘I am otherwise because of this’ … ‘I am bad because of this’ … ‘I am good because of this’ … ‘I might be because of this’ … ‘I might be here because of this’ … ‘I might be like this because of this’ … ‘I might be otherwise because of this’ … ‘May I be because of this’ … ‘May I be here because of this’ … ‘May I be like this because of this’ … ‘May I be otherwise because of this’ … ‘I will be because of this’ … ‘I will be here because of this’ … ‘I will be like this because of this’ … ‘I will be otherwise because of this.’
an4.200:16.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is not ignited. | T: (none)
an4.200:17.1 | S: And how does a mendicant burn up? | T: “And how does a monk burn?
an4.200:17.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant hasn’t given up the conceit ‘I am’, cut it off at the root, made it like a palm stump, obliterated it, so it’s unable to arise in the future. | T: There is the case where a monk’s conceit, ‘I am,’ is not abandoned, its root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising.
an4.200:17.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is burned up. | T: This is how a monk burns.
an4.200:18.1 | S: And how does a mendicant not burn up? | T: “And how does a monk not burn?
an4.200:18.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant has given up the conceit ‘I am’, cut it off at the root, made it like a palm stump, obliterated it, so it’s unable to arise in the future. | T: There is the case where a monk’s conceit, ‘I am,’ is abandoned, its root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising.
an4.200:18.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is not burned up.” | T: This is how a monk doesn’t burn.”
